[38;5;231m[49m⏺[39m [1mSection 3 — Testing and tooling[0m

  This repo has [1mno tooling at all[0m: no test runner, no linter, no formatter, no scripts, no dependencies, no CI. Since
  we're about to add a module that other code will depend on, this is the cheapest moment to decide what it's set up
  with — before there's any code to retrofit.

  But there's a concrete obstacle to unit-testing [38;5;153msession.js[39m, and it's worth understanding before you pick, because it's
  a direct consequence of choosing approach B.

  [38;5;153msession.js[39m will contain [38;5;153mexport[39m statements. [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has no [38;5;153m"type"[39m field, so Node treats every [38;5;153m.js[39m file as CommonJS
  — meaning Node would refuse to load [38;5;153msession.js[39m at all ([38;5;153mUnexpected token 'export'[39m), even though browsers load it
  happily. Testing it in Node needs one of:

  - [1mDeclare [38;5;153m"type": "module"[39m in [38;5;153mpackage.json[0m, which is the accurate description of this repo's browser code, and rename
    the two orphaned CommonJS demo files [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m / [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m to [38;5;153m.cjs[39m so they still run. Cleanest end state, but
    it touches two files unrelated to your request, so I won't do it without you saying so.
  - [1mName the shared module [38;5;153msession.mjs[0m instead. Nothing else changes. The risk is at serve time, not test time: naive
    static servers (including Python's [38;5;153mhttp.server[39m on some setups) don't map [38;5;153m.mjs[39m to a JavaScript MIME type, and
    browsers hard-refuse module scripts served with the wrong type. It'd work until it silently didn't.
  - [1mSkip Node entirely[0m and verify in a browser.

  The test logic itself is easy either way — the whole module is get/set/clear over a stubbed storage object, and the
  error paths (blocked storage, corrupt JSON, wrong shape) are exactly the kind of thing that's miserable to check by
  hand and trivial to assert.

  For the other categories: a linter/formatter is the standard low-cost win here, though it means adding dependencies to
  a repo that currently has none. End-to-end testing would need a browser driver, which is heavy for one form. Fuzz and
  mutation testing I don't think this earns.


